    { heading: "California design drivers: climate, code, and risk", body: "California licenses assisted living as residential care facilities for the elderly through the Department of Social Services — a social-services model, not a health-department one. Title 24 energy compliance, OSHPD-adjacent considerations for higher-acuity projects, and seismic detailing shape every senior project. California has no certificate-of-need program. These drivers flow directly into the MEP basis of design: HVAC equipment selection and sizing, envelope performance targets, emergency power strategy, and the structural provisions for environmental loads. A senior living building that ignores its state's dominant risk — whether that is heat, cold, wind, water, or seismic — pays for it in operating cost, insurance, or both." },
    { heading: "Engineering scope for California senior living projects", body: "Apex Grid's California senior living scope typically spans MEP (HVAC per the applicable ASHRAE standard, electrical with NEC 700/701/702 emergency architecture, plumbing with senior-specific fixture and hot-water design), structural (including any seismic, wind, or snow provisions California demands), and civil (grading, drainage, utilities, and site work for the campus). Memory care adds wandering-prevention and delayed-egress coordination; nursing components add health-care-grade systems. One coordinated team, one responsible point of contact." }
