    { heading: "Engineering senior living in Idaho Falls", body: "Idaho licenses residential assisted living facilities through the Department of Health and Welfare. Cold winters and wildfire-smoke seasons both shape HVAC design — heating performance plus filtration. The state has no certificate-of-need program for these projects. In Idaho Falls specifically, that means a mechanical basis of design tuned to local conditions, electrical with emergency and standby architecture for a non-evacuating population, plumbing designed around senior-safe bathrooms and reliable hot water, and life safety under the correct NFPA 101 occupancy chapter — confirmed with the AHJ before design development." },
    { heading: "Getting a Idaho Falls project permitted", body: "The Idaho Falls entitlement path runs through the local building department's plan check plus the Idaho Department of Health and Welfare's physical-plant review — two tracks, two checklists, best engaged in parallel. Early pre-submittal meetings that confirm occupancy classification, sprinkler standard, and submittal requirements compress the timeline more than any drafting acceleration. Apex Grid's senior living practice is built for exactly this: front-loaded decisions, coordinated disciplines, fewer correction cycles." }
