    { heading: "Louisville senior housing market", body: "Louisville the state's largest city and primary senior housing market. Louisville and Lexington anchor Kentucky's senior housing market, with the state's aging coal-country counties driving demand for affordable assisted living. Northern Kentucky feeds off Cincinnati's medical corridor. For developers, the Louisville market rewards projects that match the local demand profile — the care levels, price points, and amenity expectations of the households actually moving here — rather than a national template." },
    { heading: "Engineering senior living in Louisville", body: "Kentucky licenses assisted living through the Cabinet for Health and Family Services. Ohio River humidity and freeze cycles shape MEP design; floodplain detailing matters along the river corridors. Kentucky operates a certificate-of-need program covering nursing facility beds. In Louisville specifically, that means a mechanical basis of design tuned to local conditions, electrical with emergency and standby architecture for a non-evacuating population, plumbing designed around senior-safe bathrooms and reliable hot water, and life safety under the correct NFPA 101 occupancy chapter — confirmed with the AHJ before design development." },
    { heading: "Getting a Louisville project permitted", body: "The Louisville entitlement path runs through the local building department's plan check plus the Kentucky Cabinet for Health and Family Services's physical-plant review — two tracks, two checklists, best engaged in parallel. Early pre-submittal meetings that confirm occupancy classification, sprinkler standard, and submittal requirements compress the timeline more than any drafting acceleration. Apex Grid's senior living practice is built for exactly this: front-loaded decisions, coordinated disciplines, fewer correction cycles." }
