    { heading: "Engineering senior living in Bangor", body: "Maine licenses assisted living through the Department of Health and Human Services. Deep-winter envelopes, snow loads, freeze protection, and backup heat are non-negotiable; coastal projects add wind and corrosion detailing. Maine operates a certificate-of-need program covering nursing facility beds. In Bangor specifically, that means a mechanical basis of design tuned to local conditions, electrical with emergency and standby architecture for a non-evacuating population, plumbing designed around senior-safe bathrooms and reliable hot water, and life safety under the correct NFPA 101 occupancy chapter — confirmed with the AHJ before design development." },
    { heading: "Getting a Bangor project permitted", body: "The Bangor entitlement path runs through the local building department's plan check plus the Maine Department of Health and Human Services's physical-plant review — two tracks, two checklists, best engaged in parallel. Early pre-submittal meetings that confirm occupancy classification, sprinkler standard, and submittal requirements compress the timeline more than any drafting acceleration. Apex Grid's senior living practice is built for exactly this: front-loaded decisions, coordinated disciplines, fewer correction cycles." }
