  sections: [
    { heading: "Mitchell senior housing market", body: "Mitchell southeast regional center on the I-90 corridor. South Dakota's no-income-tax picture and Sioux Falls's medical hub have pulled retiree in-migration, while the Black Hills draw seasonal and permanent senior residents. Rural counties are aging fast with limited local options. For developers, the Mitchell market rewards projects that match the local demand profile — the care levels, price points, and amenity expectations of the households actually moving here — rather than a national template." },
    { heading: "Engineering senior living in Mitchell", body: "South Dakota licenses assisted living centers through the Department of Health. Extreme winters, blizzards, and wide temperature swings dominate MEP scope; backup-power reliability is essential. The state does not operate a certificate-of-need program for these projects. In Mitchell specifically, that means a mechanical basis of design tuned to local conditions, electrical with emergency and standby architecture for a non-evacuating population, plumbing designed around senior-safe bathrooms and reliable hot water, and life safety under the correct NFPA 101 occupancy chapter — confirmed with the AHJ before design development." },
    { heading: "Getting a Mitchell project permitted", body: "The Mitchell entitlement path runs through the local building department's plan check plus the South Dakota Department of Health's physical-plant review — two tracks, two checklists, best engaged in parallel. Early pre-submittal meetings that confirm occupancy classification, sprinkler standard, and submittal requirements compress the timeline more than any drafting acceleration. Apex Grid's senior living practice is built for exactly this: front-loaded decisions, coordinated disciplines, fewer correction cycles." }
  ],
