  h1: "Senior Living Engineering in Jackson, WY",
  answer: "Jackson resort gateway with affluent retiree appeal, making it one of Wyoming's active senior housing markets. Wyoming's no-income-tax status and Jackson Hole's resort economy draw affluent retirees, while Cheyenne and Casper serve energy-economy seniors aging in place. Projects here design to Wyoming's requirements through the Wyoming Department of Health.",
  directAnswer: "Jackson resort gateway with affluent retiree appeal. Senior living projects here follow Wyoming licensing through the Wyoming Department of Health, and Apex Grid Engineering delivers the full MEP, structural, and civil package — from occupancy analysis and entitlements through permit-ready documents, on the fast timelines developers need.",
  hook: "resort gateway with affluent retiree appeal",
  sections: [
    { heading: "Jackson senior housing market", body: "Jackson resort gateway with affluent retiree appeal. Wyoming's no-income-tax status and Jackson Hole's resort economy draw affluent retirees, while Cheyenne and Casper serve energy-economy seniors aging in place. The state's small, dispersed population makes each new assisted living project regionally significant. For developers, the Jackson market rewards projects that match the local demand profile — the care levels, price points, and amenity expectations of the households actually moving here — rather than a national template." },
    { heading: "Engineering senior living in Jackson", body: "Wyoming licenses assisted living through the Department of Health's Office of Healthcare Licensing and Surveys. Extreme winters, high winds, and altitude shape structural and MEP scope; the state does not operate a certificate-of-need program for these projects. In Jackson specifically, that means a mechanical basis of design tuned to local conditions, electrical with emergency and standby architecture for a non-evacuating population, plumbing designed around senior-safe bathrooms and reliable hot water, and life safety under the correct NFPA 101 occupancy chapter — confirmed with the AHJ before design development." },
    { heading: "Getting a Jackson project permitted", body: "The Jackson entitlement path runs through the local building department's plan check plus the Wyoming Department of Health's physical-plant review — two tracks, two checklists, best engaged in parallel. Early pre-submittal meetings that confirm occupancy classification, sprinkler standard, and submittal requirements compress the timeline more than any drafting acceleration. Apex Grid's senior living practice is built for exactly this: front-loaded decisions, coordinated disciplines, fewer correction cycles." }
  ],
